The rule uses a maintenance-wave idea: it estimates how many duties the rest of the fleet can absorb during one robot’s maintenance and lets a robot run ahead of the fair workload only by that dynamic lead. Within that lead it pushes the most maintenance-advanced robot; outside it, it balances counts/hours. A coverage guard avoids triggering maintenance when no other robot can cover, and new robot types are ranked by effective duty fraction including amortized maintenance.

