The key improvement is to avoid triggering maintenance when the currently opened robots have enough remaining work budget to cover the entire remaining horizon. The original rule only avoided maintenance when the remaining horizon was shorter than the maintenance duration, but even with a long horizon we can often finish without any maintenance if total remaining budget suffices. By computing the sum of `duties_left * work_time` across all opened robots and setting a flag `avoid_maint` when this capacity is at least `ctx.remaining`, we prevent unnecessary maintenance events. This reduces resource usage (no extra robots needed to cover maintenance gaps) and improves imbalance (all first-maintenance times remain `None`). The rest of the rule remains unchanged, preserving its effective load balancing and staggering logic when maintenance is truly unavoidable.

